  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 8.1. Increased capacity of member States to develop new or reformed sustainable national social protection strategies, policies or legal frameworks to extend coverage and enhance benefit adequacy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">(1) Draft legislation for the establishment of an Unemployment Insurance (UI) Fund has been developed by the Ministry of Labor (MOL) and the National Social Security Fund (NSSF), in consultation with social partners. A draft design in line with ILS was discussed in a tripartite dialogue meeting in April 2021. Draft law has been submitted by MOL and NSSF (through selected MPs) to parliament committee for discussion in September 2021.




(2)The Office of the Prime Minister of the caretaker government approved the implementation of the COVID-19 emergency cash assistance scheme, the National Social Solidarity Program (NSSP) in March 2020. The NSSP provides income support in the form of direct cash payments to households affected by Covid-19. The NSSP has been implemented over various phases which have benefitted from adaptations to improve its reach and delivery.  The NSSP includes categorical eligibility criteria  to identify people experiencing lifecycle vulnerabilities, such as persons with disability and older people. Initial estimates suggest that approximately 290,000 households were considered eligible to receive a benefit. A report documenting the design and implementation of the program, and policy recommendations, has been prepared in collaboration with the Lebanese Army Forces in charge of implementation. (1)The ILO has provided ongoing technical assistance to the Ministry of Labor and the National Social Security Fund (NSSF) on the establishment of UI fund, including a costing exercise, tripartite dialogue launched in April 2021, and the review and recommendations on the draft UI legislation. 
The ILO provided technical assistance; developing the terms of reference with NSSF and MoL, and securing the actuarial and financial audit expertise needed to design and conduct the assessment for the NSSF. 
Upon the request of the NSSF, an assessment of its financial position and short-term financial risks is being conducted in 2021. The assessment is expected to provide policy recommendations to preserve short-term financial balance in line with ILO international social security standards.

(2)The ILO provided technical assistance to the government in the design of the NSSP and the need to include categorical targeting to provide coverage for lifecycle contingencies, in addition to the documentation of the process. The report  prepared by the ILO provides policy recommendations for future cash assistance programs.</narrative>
   </description>
